WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.269 --> 00:00:07.610
Every year on July 14, France celebrates Bastille Day.

2
00:00:08.110 --> 00:00:15.972
Parades fill the streets of Paris; French soldiers march down its most famous avenue, the Champs-Élysées; and the

3
00:00:16.012 --> 00:00:18.793
country's politicians speak to cheering crowds.

4
00:00:19.573 --> 00:00:27.175
The holiday commemorates the day in 1789 when a crowd of French citizens stormed the Bastille, an old

5
00:00:27.255 --> 00:00:29.136
fortress being used as a prison.

6
00:00:29.960 --> 00:00:34.922
Why do the French celebrate an attack on a poorly guarded and nearly empty prison?

7
00:00:35.562 --> 00:00:41.105
They celebrate it because the fall of the Bastille marks the start of the French Revolution.

8
00:00:41.925 --> 00:00:47.728
Before the French Revolution, King Louis XVI ruled France in an absolute monarchy.

9
00:00:48.328 --> 00:00:53.470
With the support of its nobles and the Catholic Church, he had total control of the country.

10
00:00:54.130 --> 00:01:01.177
The people of France were legally organized into three social classes: clergy, nobility, and commoners.

11
00:01:01.757 --> 00:01:07.979
The clergy and nobility made up about 3 percent of the population and held the highest positions in

12
00:01:08.019 --> 00:01:10.379
the church, the army, and the government.

13
00:01:11.039 --> 00:01:18.161
The clergy collected about 10 percent of French citizens' incomes and production but did not pay taxes on

14
00:01:18.201 --> 00:01:19.982
their own extensive land holdings.

15
00:01:20.782 --> 00:01:26.564
The nobles collected rent and taxes from the peasants who lived on their lands but paid no taxes

16
00:01:26.624 --> 00:01:27.904
to the crown themselves.

17
00:01:28.508 --> 00:01:32.790
The remaining 97 percent of the population of France were commoners.

18
00:01:33.531 --> 00:01:38.634
This group consisted of peasants and a growing middle class called the bourgeoisie.

19
00:01:39.174 --> 00:01:45.177
The commoners usually paid a substantial portion of their incomes to the nobles, the clergy, and the crown.

20
00:01:46.058 --> 00:01:49.500
But in the eighteenth century, French society was changing.

21
00:01:50.060 --> 00:01:55.003
Many of its members wanted to create a government run by the people instead of a monarch.

22
00:01:55.443 --> 00:02:00.547
The members of the bourgeoisie wanted to have more rights and pay fewer taxes.

23
00:02:01.207 --> 00:02:05.311
They had become wealthy in business, but could not hold titles or land.

24
00:02:05.931 --> 00:02:10.035
They also did not have the hereditary privileges allowed to the nobility.

25
00:02:10.675 --> 00:02:15.919
Furthermore, the bourgeoisie was well educated but had little say in running the country.

26
00:02:16.540 --> 00:02:19.562
A growing intellectual movement fueled their resentment.

27
00:02:20.163 --> 00:02:27.132
The writers and thinkers of the Enlightenment believed in the ideals of reason, freedom, progress, and greater rights

28
00:02:27.192 --> 00:02:27.933
for commoners.

29
00:02:28.773 --> 00:02:33.155
France's poor economy in the 1780s also encouraged revolution.

30
00:02:33.775 --> 00:02:36.036
The country was on the brink of bankruptcy.

31
00:02:36.817 --> 00:02:44.120
Several recent wars, as well as support for the American Revolution against Britain, had depleted the country's finances.

32
00:02:44.760 --> 00:02:53.024
Harsh winters and failed crops had led to famines, and basic foods—bread in particular—were unavailable or unaffordable.

33
00:02:53.704 --> 00:02:58.649
The high levels of unemployment plaguing the country only aggravated the situation.

34
00:02:59.329 --> 00:03:03.053
But France's royalty did not react to the plight of its commoners.

35
00:03:04.194 --> 00:03:12.481
In 1787, to ease the financial crisis, Louis XVI's finance minister proposed a tax on property owned by

36
00:03:12.501 --> 00:03:13.742
the nobles and clergy.

37
00:03:14.403 --> 00:03:20.840
They rejected the tax and, instead, called for a rare assembly of the Estates-General.

38
00:03:20.860 --> 00:03:28.064
This meeting consisted of representatives of the clergy (the First Estate), the nobility (the Second Estate), and the

39
00:03:28.124 --> 00:03:29.925
commoners (the Third Estate).

40
00:03:30.646 --> 00:03:37.289
As news of the coming assembly spread, people's hopes for reform grew, and lists of grievances were sent

41
00:03:37.329 --> 00:03:39.191
to the king from across the country.

42
00:03:40.151 --> 00:03:48.556
In May 1789 at the Palace of Versailles, the lavish home of Louis XVI, the Estates-General finally convened

43
00:03:48.876 --> 00:03:50.678
but quickly came to a deadlock.

44
00:03:51.258 --> 00:03:57.824
The Third Estate represented over 90 percent of the population and an increasing amount of France's wealth.

45
00:03:58.484 --> 00:04:03.609
As a result, its members insisted that their votes be given more weight than in the past.

46
00:04:04.289 --> 00:04:09.714
The First and Second Estates refused to make the change, and no decisions were made for weeks.

47
00:04:10.615 --> 00:04:17.824
On June 17, 1789, the Third Estate decided to proceed in its task without the input of the

48
00:04:17.884 --> 00:04:18.965
clergy and nobles.

49
00:04:19.585 --> 00:04:25.466
It declared itself a National Assembly, with full authority to resolve France's financial troubles.

50
00:04:26.347 --> 00:04:33.128
In response, the king quickly closed the assembly hall where the National Assembly met, but its members continued

51
00:04:33.188 --> 00:04:34.989
to meet in an indoor tennis court.

52
00:04:35.709 --> 00:04:41.831
There, they all pledged an oath not to separate until they had created a new constitution for France.

53
00:04:42.791 --> 00:04:46.312
The people of Paris were following the events at Versailles closely.

54
00:04:46.984 --> 00:04:53.226
When they learned that Louis XVI had ordered the army to gather around Versailles and Paris, they feared

55
00:04:53.266 --> 00:04:57.007
the king was about to take control of the National Assembly by force.

56
00:04:57.748 --> 00:05:05.530
News of the army's arrival incited riots in the streets of Paris, and on July 14, 1789, a

57
00:05:05.610 --> 00:05:07.151
mob approached the Bastille.

58
00:05:07.751 --> 00:05:11.352
The old fortress was where France's royalty imprisoned its enemies.

59
00:05:12.032 --> 00:05:18.176
To Parisians, it was a symbol of tyranny and oppression by an uncaring government that did not answer

60
00:05:18.216 --> 00:05:18.876
to anyone.

61
00:05:19.557 --> 00:05:25.000
It also contained a supply of gunpowder and weapons that would be useful for the revolutionaries.

62
00:05:25.860 --> 00:05:32.204
As the leaders of the mob negotiated with the Bastille's commander, shots were fired and fighting broke out.

63
00:05:32.825 --> 00:05:38.128
The mob broke into the fortress, freed its seven prisoners, and seized the weapons within.

64
00:05:39.080 --> 00:05:44.144
As a message to the king, the crowd hacked off the commander's head and displayed it on the

65
00:05:44.244 --> 00:05:45.044
end of a spear.

66
00:05:45.665 --> 00:05:47.506
The French Revolution had begun.

67
00:05:48.046 --> 00:05:52.089
The storming of the Bastille showed the people's support for the National Assembly.

68
00:05:52.690 --> 00:05:57.093
The uprising spread across France, and many nobles fled the country.

69
00:05:57.773 --> 00:06:02.897
In August, the assembly published the "Declaration of the Rights of Man and of the Citizen."

70
00:06:03.417 --> 00:06:06.920
The main principles were liberty, equality, and fraternity.

71
00:06:07.684 --> 00:06:13.769
All French people were now subject to the same laws, had the same privileges, and could no longer

72
00:06:13.829 --> 00:06:15.290
be imprisoned without cause.

73
00:06:16.191 --> 00:06:19.734
The years after the storming of the Bastille were full of unrest.

74
00:06:20.315 --> 00:06:23.677
Political parties formed and battled for control of the country.

75
00:06:24.298 --> 00:06:30.603
Power struggles continued as the assembly strove to establish a new constitution and a working government.

76
00:06:31.224 --> 00:06:36.268
In 1792, the monarchy had ended, and a new republic had been born.

77
00:06:36.940 --> 00:06:43.562
France went to war with Austria, Prussia, Britain, and Spain, nations whose monarchs were loyal to the king

78
00:06:43.602 --> 00:06:44.162
of France.

79
00:06:44.922 --> 00:06:52.584
The turmoil within France reached a horrific extreme when a revolutionary named Maximilien Robespierre gained power.

80
00:06:53.465 --> 00:06:59.206
Under the leadership of Robespierre, France entered a yearlong period known as the Reign of Terror.

81
00:06:59.886 --> 00:07:05.428
Anyone could be sentenced to death on the slightest suspicion of being against the National Assembly.

82
00:07:06.024 --> 00:07:15.327
Louis XVI was beheaded for treason on January 21, 1793, and Marie Antoinette was beheaded nine months later.

83
00:07:15.887 --> 00:07:24.669
From 1793 to 1794, as many as twenty thousand people were led through jeering mobs and executed at

84
00:07:24.709 --> 00:07:25.389
the guillotine.

85
00:07:26.129 --> 00:07:31.031
Fearing that they would be next, the leaders of the revolution began accusing one another.

86
00:07:31.711 --> 00:07:37.833
Robespierre himself was put to death, and a new government, called the Directory, came into power.

87
00:07:38.614 --> 00:07:40.895
The new government failed to unite the country.

88
00:07:41.595 --> 00:07:47.959
In 1799, the general of France's army, Napoleon Bonaparte, overthrew the Directory.

89
00:07:48.539 --> 00:07:52.741
He became a dictator and declared himself emperor in 1804.

90
00:07:53.482 --> 00:07:59.885
It would take many, many more years of war and political struggle before the republic's ideals of liberty,

91
00:08:00.045 --> 00:08:02.326
equality, and fraternity were realized.

92
00:08:02.907 --> 00:08:06.009
The ideal of democracy, however, had taken hold.

93
00:08:06.610 --> 00:08:12.716
The French Revolution's dramatic and bloody overthrow of the monarchy signaled that the will of the people should

94
00:08:12.776 --> 00:08:13.657
not be ignored.

95
00:08:14.338 --> 00:08:21.125
The principles promoted by this revolution have inspired people all over the world to fight for freedom and

96
00:08:21.205 --> 00:08:21.866
human rights.
